BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2021, "n": 8, "part": "B", "topic": "graph", "title": "Travelling Trucks", "answer": 2, "story": "<p>Trucks travel between six cities using the roads shown in the\ndiagram. Each road has a bridge or tunnel that limits the height of a\ntruck that can travel along it. The maximum truck height for each road\nis indicated in the diagram.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc4')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Travelling Trucks<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc4\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>Four cities form the vertices of a hexagon with top and bottom horizontal sides. Moving around the hexagon, there are two top cities (top-left and top-right vertices), a city on the right (right vertex), two bottom cities (bottom-right and bottom-left vertices), and a city on the left (left vertex).<\/p>\n<p>The left city is labelled Start and the right city is labelled Finish. The roads with bridges and tunnels between the cities, along with their maximum heights, are given below.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Between left (Start) and top-left: Bridge, 180<\/li>\n<li>Between left (Start) and bottom-left: Bridge, 200<\/li>\n<li>Between top-left and top-right: Bridge, 50<\/li>\n<li>Between top-left and bottom-left: Tunnel, 70<\/li>\n<li>Between top-left and bottom-right: Bridge, 90<\/li>\n<li>Between bottom-left and bottom-right: Bridge, 80<\/li>\n<li>Between top-right and bottom-right: Tunnel, 95<\/li>\n<li>Between top-right and right (Finish): Bridge, 100<\/li>\n<li>Between bottom-right and right (Finish): Bridge, 75<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>What is the maximum height of a truck that can travel from Start to\nFinish?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>70<\/li>\n<li>80<\/li>\n<li>90<\/li>\n<li>95<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(C) 90<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>In order to travel from Start to Finish, a truck must pass from the\ngroup of three cities on the left to the group of three cities on the\nright.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The left, top-left, and bottom-left cities are circled forming a group including the Start. The right, top-right and bottom right cities are circled forming a group including the Finish.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>To do so, the truck must take one of the three middle roads, with\nmaximum heights of 50, 90, and 80. This tells us that a truck with a\nheight greater than 90 cannot make the desired trip.<\/p>\n<p>Now it remains to verify that a truck of height 90 can make the\ndesired trip. One option is for such a truck to take the path indicated\nin the following diagram.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A path following the roads as follows: From the left city that is marked Start, the path goes to the top-left city (max 180), then to bottom-right city (max 90), then to the top-right city (max 95), then to right city (max 100) that is marked Finish.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Notice that each road on the indicated path has a maximum height of\nat least 90.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>This task is a a representation of how data flows through a\n<span><em>computer network<\/em><\/span>. In a computer network, whether\nit is connected via wifi or a wires, there are capacity limits on how\nmuch data can flow between two points, which is known as the\n<span><em>bandwidth<\/em><\/span> between two points. These bandwidth can\nbe on the wireless connection, the physical wires, or the\n<span><em>routers<\/em><\/span> which direct <span><em>data\npackets<\/em><\/span> through the network.<\/p>\n<p>In this task, each bridge or tunnel represents the bandwidth through\na given point, and the key problem to solve is what route has enough\nbandwidth to support the amount of information that needs to flow.<\/p>"});